---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/portsea.json";
const totalCtrl = d.control.rows.reduce((a, r) => a + r[2], 0);
---
<Base
  title="Two years of Portsea"
  eyebrow={`${d.parish} · coverage tested ${d.searched}`}
  dek="The first record of this family ever found in England survives because it falls inside a transcript twenty-two months long. Nobody had checked how long the transcript was.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">On 19 March 1780, at Portsea St Mary in Hampshire, a child called
    <strong>Joseph D'Arcy</strong> was baptised, son of <strong>Robert</strong> and
    <strong>Jean</strong>. It is the earliest English record this archive holds of the family, and it
    is the only document anywhere that names
    <a href={u("/people/robert-d-arcy/")}>Major-General Robert D'Arcy</a> as somebody's father.
    <a href={u("/hornby/")}>How it was found →</a></p>

    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem">This page is not about that entry. It is about how much
    of Portsea's register stands behind it, which is a question that should have been asked first.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The control</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Five common surnames, six years, one parish</h2>

    <p>{d.control.method}</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th class="m">Hampshire, 1778–1783</th><th class="m">Of those, Portsea St Mary</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.control.rows.map(([sn, ham, ports]) => (
            <tr><td><strong>{sn}</strong></td><td class="m">{ham}</td><td class="m"><strong>{ports}</strong></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">That is <strong>{totalCtrl} Portsea entries</strong>. Here is when
    they fall.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th class="m">Entries</th><th>&nbsp;</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.control.years.map(([y, n]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="m"><strong>{y}</strong></td>
              <td class="m">{n}</td>
              <td><span style={`display:inline-block;height:9px;border-radius:2px;background:${n ? "var(--accent)" : "var(--rule)"};width:${Math.max(n * 9, 3)}px`}></span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.9rem">{d.control.types}</p>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">{d.earlier}</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <strong>{d.verdict}</strong>
      <span class="chip doc">coverage established</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What that means</h2>
    <ol class="dek">
      {d.consequences.map(([h, b]) => (
        <li style="margin-bottom:.7rem"><strong>{h}</strong> {b}</li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <strong>Answered the next day, 13 September 2026.</strong> FindMyPast's Hampshire Baptisms
      covers Portsea St Mary continuously — a control returns entries for 1770, 1771, 1772, 1774,
      1775, 1779, 1782, 1785, 1786, 1788, 1789, 1790, 1791 and 1792 — and every D'Arcy spelling
      across that window returns Joseph and nobody else. The silence is real after all. The same
      search found Robert D'Arcy's marriage to Jean Ward at this church on 21 June 1779.
      <a href={u("/behind-the-paywall/")}>Both →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>The find is not weakened. The silence around it is.</strong> Joseph D'Arcy was baptised
      at Portsea on 19 March 1780 to Robert and Jean, and that remains as solid as it was this
      morning — it is a parish register entry and it says what it says. What has changed is that this
      archive can no longer treat Portsea as a parish it has searched. It has searched
      twenty-two months of it. <a href={u("/errands/")}>The volume to read →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Filed as an error →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
